WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.600 line:10%
Ever wonder how a muscle actually shortens?

2
00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.439 line:10%
At rest, myosin has already used ATP to reach a cocked,

3
00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:09.203 line:10%
high energy state, but tropomyosin is still

4
00:00:09.203 --> 00:00:10.398 line:10%
blocking its path.

5
00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.792 line:10%
Zooming in, the ATP splits into ADP and

6
00:00:12.792 --> 00:00:15.321 line:10%
phosphate, placing the myosin head in its

7
00:00:15.321 --> 00:00:17.199 line:10%
cocked, high energy position.

8
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:19.795 line:10%
Calcium floods in and binds troponin,

9
00:00:19.795 --> 00:00:21.999 line:10%
shifting tropomyosin out of the way.

10
00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.414 line:10%
Now myosin grabs actin and swings the power stroke,

11
00:00:25.414 --> 00:00:27.911 line:10%
releasing phosphate right as it pulls,

12
00:00:27.911 --> 00:00:29.878 line:10%
dragging the filament inward.

13
00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:32.920 line:10%
A fresh ATP binds and myosin lets go of actin,

14
00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.000 line:10%
resetting for the next stroke.

15
00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.902 line:10%
Cycle after cycle, the filaments slide further in,

16
00:00:37.902 --> 00:00:39.559 line:10%
and the whole muscle shortens.

17
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:47.149 line:10%
Here's the full cycle at a glance,

18
00:00:47.149 --> 00:00:50.919 line:10%
the nucleotide bound and the head's position at every stage.

19
00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:52.593 line:10%
Three steps: ATP cocks

20
00:00:52.593 --> 00:00:54.822 line:10%
the head, calcium opens the site,

21
00:00:54.822 --> 00:00:58.405 line:10%
and the power stroke pulls actin in, and repeating that

22
00:00:58.405 --> 00:00:59.838 line:10%
shortens the muscle.

